I have a historical dataset of 1,000+ Job Requisition (JR) records in this Excel workbook.

I want to use the historical actual joining dates to statistically determine the most accurate “Proposed Days to Fill” for each Candidate Progress Status.

The objective is NOT simply to calculate the average number of days.

The objective is to determine the number of proposed days that gives the highest accuracy when predicting the ACTUAL JOINING MONTH.

DATA COLUMNS

The relevant columns are:

* Actual Joining Date
* Job Requisition ID
* Candidate Progress Status
* JR Modified Date
* Country
* Internal Job Watch

Additional columns may exist in the workbook. Use them only if they were known at the time the proposed joining date would have been estimated.

Interpret Internal Job Watch as:

* TRUE = Internal hiring
* FALSE = External hiring

For geography:

* Country = India → India
* Any country other than India → ROW (Rest of World)

STEP 1 — DATA VALIDATION

First inspect the dataset and report:

1. Total number of records.
2. Number of records with missing Actual Joining Date.
3. Number of records with missing JR Modified Date.
4. Number of records with missing Candidate Progress Status.
5. Number of records with missing Country.
6. Number of records with missing Internal Job Watch.
7. Number of duplicate Job Requisition IDs.
8. Any invalid or inconsistent dates.
9. Any Candidate Progress Status values that appear to be duplicates because of spelling, capitalization, spaces, or minor naming differences.

Do NOT silently modify the source data.

Create a cleaned/calculation layer or additional columns instead.

STEP 2 — CALCULATE ACTUAL DAYS TO FILL

For every valid historical record calculate:

Actual Days to Fill =
Actual Joining Date - JR Modified Date

Do not round the result.

Also calculate:

Actual Joining Month =
MONTH(Actual Joining Date)

Actual Joining Year =
YEAR(Actual Joining Date)

Actual Joining Year-Month =
YEAR(Actual Joining Date) + MONTH(Actual Joining Date)

Also create:

Geography =
IF(Country = “India”, “India”, “ROW”)

Hiring Type =
IF(Internal Job Watch = TRUE, “Internal”, “External”)

STEP 3 — CREATE THE FOUR SEGMENTS

Every record must belong to exactly one of these four segments:

1. India + Internal
2. India + External
3. ROW + Internal
4. ROW + External

Do not combine these four segments when calculating the final proposed-day mappings.

STEP 4 — ANALYSE HISTORICAL ACTUAL DAYS

For every combination of:

Segment + Candidate Progress Status

calculate:

* Number of records
* Mean Actual Days to Fill
* Median Actual Days to Fill
* Minimum Actual Days
* Maximum Actual Days
* P25
* P75
* Standard deviation

The median is important because outliers should not disproportionately influence the proposed days.

STEP 5 — DETERMINE THE OPTIMAL PROPOSED DAYS

For every:

Segment + Candidate Progress Status

test different Proposed Days values.

For example, test integer values from 0 to 240 days, or a wider range if the historical data shows that some statuses require more than 240 days.

For each candidate Proposed Days value calculate:

Estimated Joining Date =
JR Modified Date + Proposed Days

Then calculate:

Estimated Joining Month =
MONTH(Estimated Joining Date)

Estimated Joining Year =
YEAR(Estimated Joining Date)

Estimated Joining Year-Month =
YEAR(Estimated Joining Date) + MONTH(Estimated Joining Date)

Compare Estimated Joining Year-Month against Actual Joining Year-Month.

Define:

Month Accurate =
TRUE if Estimated Joining Year-Month = Actual Joining Year-Month
FALSE otherwise.

Then calculate:

Month Accuracy % =
Number of Month Accurate records / Total records

The PRIMARY objective is:

MAXIMIZE MONTH ACCURACY.

Therefore, select the Proposed Days value that produces the highest historical joining-month accuracy for that Segment + Candidate Progress Status combination.

STEP 6 — TIE-BREAKING RULE

If multiple Proposed Days values produce the same highest month accuracy, use the following tie-breakers in this order:

1. Lowest Mean Absolute Error in days between Estimated Joining Date and Actual Joining Date.
2. Lowest Median Absolute Error in days.
3. Prefer the value closest to the historical median Actual Days to Fill.

Do not arbitrarily choose a value.

STEP 7 — AVOID OVERFITTING

Because the same historical data is being used to determine the proposed days, do not rely only on in-sample accuracy.

Where practical, divide the historical records into:

* 70% training data
* 30% validation/test data

Use the training data to determine the optimal Proposed Days.

Then apply those Proposed Days to the validation/test data and calculate the actual joining-month accuracy.

Report BOTH:

* Training accuracy
* Validation/Test accuracy

If there are too few records for a particular Segment + Candidate Progress Status combination, clearly flag it as LOW SAMPLE SIZE.

Use a minimum sample-size threshold of 10 records where possible.

For combinations with fewer than 10 records, do not create an unreliable highly specific mapping. Instead flag them and recommend using the appropriate fallback mapping based on the broader segment.

